 {"id":"CF-173","title":"P3 \u2014 the layer-00 reach probe reports 000 (unreachable) for a URL that answered 200: the CF-037 fix shape discards curl's printed code when --max-time cuts the transfer","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:35:13Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:35:13Z","detail":{"lane":"code-factory-dogfood","tick":"code-factory-dogfood-2026-11-04-07-25-03","evidence":"~/.hermes/stand-in/dogfood/code-factory/dogfood-2026-11-04-evidence.md#3"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 at published tip eada934 with a portable fixture (no external network): a local `python3 -m http.server` serving a 100 KB body, probed with `curl --limit-rate 20k --max-time 1`. Result: `curl_printed_code=200 curl_rc=28 script_value=000`. scripts/project-node.sh:190-195 (the shape CF-037 introduced) is `if ! c=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 8 \"$u\" 2>/dev/null); then c=000; fi`: curl writes the status it already received (200) and then exits 28 when --max-time cuts the body, and the `if !` throws that printed value away and records 000. The FAIL row then reads `reach:<url> \u2014 -> 000`, which is indistinguishable from DNS failure or no egress. Observed in the wild on 2026-11-03's node: layer 00 printed `FAIL reach:https://pypi.org/simple/ \u2014 -> 000` in the same run whose layer 40 printed `PASS repo_clone \u2026 @ df40870`, and three direct probes on that node returned http=200 at 9.4-10.0 s, aborted mid-body. A stranger's next move is to chase DNS or egress on a node that just cloned over the same network, and the FAIL names no value to supply. Distinct from CF-037 (the `|| echo 000` deformation, fixed) and CF-072 (that deformation in three other scripts): this is the replacement idiom's false-negative mode, and it fires on a REACHABLE URL.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the pre-fix tree from the repo root: `port=18932; d=$(mktemp -d); dd if=/dev/zero of=\"$d/big\" bs=1k count=100 status=none; ( cd \"$d\" && exec python3 -m http.server \"$port\" --bind 127.0.0.1 >/dev/null 2>&1 ) & srv=$!; sleep 1; raw=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 1 --limit-rate 20k \"http://127.0.0.1:$port/big\"); raw_rc=$?; if ! c=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 1 --limit-rate 20k \"http://127.0.0.1:$port/big\" 2>/dev/null); then c=000; fi; kill \"$srv\" 2>/dev/null; rm -rf \"$d\"; echo \"curl_printed_code=$raw curl_rc=$raw_rc script_value=$c\"` prints curl_printed_code=200 curl_rc=28 script_value=000.\n1. The probe keeps the code curl printed and uses curl's exit status only to distinguish 'no code printed' from 'code printed but the transfer was cut', so the fixture arm above yields 200 (or an explicit timeout word) and never 000.\n2. A closed port still reports 000 (no regression on the genuinely unreachable arm).\n3. tests/selftest.sh gains a fixture covering the mid-body-timeout arm that fails on the pre-fix tree and passes on the fixed tree, and explicitly rejects the token '000' for that arm.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS with rc 0 and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints RESULT: OK.","probe":"port=18932; d=$(mktemp -d); dd if=/dev/zero of=\"$d/big\" bs=1k count=100 status=none; ( cd \"$d\" && exec python3 -m http.server \"$port\" --bind 127.0.0.1 >/dev/null 2>&1 ) & srv=$!; sleep 1; raw=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 1 --limit-rate 20k \"http://127.0.0.1:$port/big\"); raw_rc=$?; if ! c=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 1 --limit-rate 20k \"http://127.0.0.1:$port/big\" 2>/dev/null); then c=000; fi; kill \"$srv\" 2>/dev/null; rm -rf \"$d\"; echo \"curl_printed_code=$raw curl_rc=$raw_rc script_value=$c\""}
