n be emitted for any gate.\n2. A working binary still yields `PASS version \u2014 <version string>` with a non-empty value.\n3. tests/selftest.sh gains a section asserting both arms and failing on the pre-fix tree.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS with rc 0 and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints RESULT: OK.","probe":"h=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$h/bin\"; printf '#!/bin/sh\\nexit 1\\n' > \"$h/bin/hermes\"; chmod +x \"$h/bin/hermes\"; HOME=\"$h\" PATH=\"$h/bin:$PATH\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=python3.12 bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --check 2>&1 | grep version"}
 {"id":"CF-175","title":"P5 — the deployed lane prompt for code-factory-duckbrain-pm names docs/PRD-code-factory-duckbrain.md, a file no ref has ever carried","status":"pending","priority":"P5","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:06Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:06Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"7ef895920d2e5aec31a091ec6fbec88b4a2d02b11331825ff283d93577e3af7d"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-pm tick (07-36-30) from a clone of origin/main at bc68e59. The lane prompt this tick received instructs \"Read `docs/PRD-code-factory-duckbrain.md` and the specs\"; this repository ships `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` and nothing else. Repository half, reproduced here: `git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/ | grep -i prd` prints `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` and `docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh`; `git log --all --oneline -- docs/PRD-code-factory-duckbrain.md | wc -l` = 0, so that path has never existed on any ref in this clone's history; `git grep -c 'PRD-code-factory-duckbrain' -- .` prints nothing (exit 1), so no tracked file carries the phantom path either. The tracked lane prompt `prompts/pm.md:7` names the real file, so the DEPLOYED prompt has drifted from the copy the repository ships: the lane was renamed to `code-factory-duckbrain` in its title and that rename was applied to the prompt's prose without re-pointing the `docs/` path. Effect: every PM tick that follows its prompt literally opens a path that cannot exist, then has to recover by searching `docs/`, and a reader cannot tell from the prompt whether the PRD was renamed or the prompt is stale — the ambiguity this lane exists to remove. The fix is one token in the scheduler's stored prompt for that lane (operator-side, not reproducible from this repository); no repository file needs to change, because `prompts/pm.md` is already correct and is the copy the divergence is measured against. Distinct from every open row: `grep -c 'PRD-code-factory-duckbrain' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` = 0 before this row was filed.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce the repository half at any tip: `git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/ | grep -i prd` lists exactly `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` and `docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh`; `git log --all --oneline -- docs/PRD-code-factory-duckbrain.md | wc -l` prints 0; `git grep -c 'PRD-code-factory-duckbrain' -- .` prints nothing (exit 1).\n1. The deployed prompt for the lane names `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` and contains zero occurrences of `PRD-code-factory-duckbrain.md`. Operator-side: read the scheduler's stored prompt for the exact lane name and grep both strings, printing both counts (expect 1 and 0). Stranger-side, the same token is already correct at `prompts/pm.md:7` — `grep -c 'docs/PRD-code-factory.md' prompts/pm.md` = 1 and `grep -c 'PRD-code-factory-duckbrain' prompts/pm.md` = 0 at the tip this row was filed.\n2. The two copies agree outside the project name: diff the deployed prompt against `prompts/pm.md` and require no difference in any `docs/...` path (a path-only diff must be empty), so the next rename cannot re-introduce the drift silently.","probe":"printf 'ls-tree: '; git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/ | grep -i prd; printf 'phantom_commits: '; git log --all --oneline -- docs/PRD-code-factory-duckbrain.md | wc -l; printf 'phantom_in_code_or_docs: '; git grep -c 'PRD-code-factory-duckbrain' -- docs prompts scripts tests specs README.md ; printf 'pm_md_real_path: '; grep -c 'docs/PRD-code-factory.md' prompts/pm.md; printf 'pm_md_phantom: '; grep -c 'PRD-code-factory-duckbrain' prompts/pm.md; true"}
+{"id":"CF-176","title":"P3 - docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md asserts \"every row enabled\" for the census it just corrected, while the row it names (code-factory-qa) was auto-disabled eight minutes before the measurement","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"70593e7aa729859e68e253c0b742cbc5a080a95803a89c831e1bb506fc0f5a0d"},"reasoning":"docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md:55, committed by the CF-169 correction (6fd2279), tells an operator that the family census it just corrected ends \"every row enabled\". The row it names in that same sentence — `qa`, one of the five 8x ratios — has been enabled=0 since 2026-11-04T06:03:49Z with disabled_by=auto-disable and disabled_reason \"failure rate 91.3% (84/92 ticks, window 100, threshold 0.90)\", which is seven minutes BEFORE the docs tick that measured the census (code-factory-docs-2026-11-04-06-11-13). The same sentence is repeated in the closed row's evidence artifact section 3 (\"Every one of those rows is enabled=1\"). The ratios themselves reproduce exactly: the failure is the enabled count beside them, and it is the one sentence an operator would use to audit whether the family is armed.\n\nThis is the CF-157 class (a shipped document asserting all lanes enabled=true while the census prints code-factory-qa enabled=false) reappearing in a different file after CF-157 closed with a README-scoped correction. It is distinct from CF-157 because that row's fix is complete and README-only, and from CF-134 because the wrong value here is the enabled state, not the pacing ratio. Nothing gates the class: the corrected paragraph was written eight minutes after the auto-disable, and the gate (tests/selftest.sh) reads neither the doc nor the DB.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: `grep -n 'every row enabled' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md` prints `55:` with the sentence, and `sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name,enabled,disabled_by,disabled_reason from projects where name like 'code-factory%' and cooldown_ceiling_s>0 and enabled=0\"` prints `code-factory-qa|0|auto-disable|failure rate 91.3% (84/92 ticks, window 100, threshold 0.90)` — the sentence is contradicted by the same family the paragraph cites, and `code-factory-qa` is one of the five 8x rows it names.\n1. The census sentence no longer asserts the enabled state in the present tense: it either names the measurement timestamp and the counted value (16 of 17 enabled at 2026-11-04T06:11Z), or names the disabled row with its `disabled_by`/`disabled_reason`. `grep -c 'every row enabled' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md` prints 0.\n2. The replacement carries ONE re-runnable query that prints the ratios and the enabled count together, and the sentence quotes its output for the disabled row, so the two numbers cannot drift apart again the way the ratio count and the enabled count just did.\n3. The same claim is corrected or annotated in the closed row's evidence artifact `docs/audits/code-factory-docs-2026-11-04-cf169-validator-claim.md`, whose section 3 states \"Every one of those rows is `enabled=1`\" — either the sentence is fixed there or that file says it is superseded by this row, so the closure evidence stops carrying a false measurement.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0 and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the fix commit (documentation-only change; no selftest arm is expected to change).","probe":"grep -n 'every row enabled' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md; sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name, enabled, disabled_by, disabled_reason from projects where name like 'code-factory%' and cooldown_ceiling_s>0 and enabled=0;\""}
+{"id":"CF-177","title":"P4 - the corrected admission paragraph pins another repository's line ranges but no scheduler commit, and laneAutoArm's cited range ends on the file's last line","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":1,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"b6332f6e6041090c57d70962bf305bbf6ca0898b9dc11819bd59f1a9def84297"},"reasoning":"The corrected paragraph cites the scheduler's source by bare line ranges (internal/api/lane_onboarding.go:269-271, :303-310, :389-403 and internal/database/models.go:39,47-52) into a DIFFERENT repository, and names no commit anywhere in the shipped file: the pin exists only in the evidence artifact (bf7427c8). Every number in that sentence is therefore only reproducible while coding-hermes/scheduler origin/main happens to equal the tree the docs tick read — which is true today and is luck, not a contract. laneAutoArm's cited range 389-403 is the whole tail of a 403-line file, so the first function appended after it silently re-assigns the citation to other code. The reviewer who re-checks the paragraph a month from now has two failure modes and no way to tell which one they are in.\n\nDistinct from CF-095, where the artifact is pinned and the tip it names fails to reproduce: here the artifact is pinned and the shipped document is the unpinned one. This is the documentation-provenance gap the repo's own traceability doctrine asks for (row id, spec, test, evidence, verdict), applied to a citation that lives outside the repo.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: `grep -c 'lane_onboarding\\.go' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md` prints `2` and `grep -cE '(bf7427c8|scheduler@[0-9a-f]{7})' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md` prints `0`, so the corrected paragraph pins exact line ranges inside another repository while naming no commit that a reader can check them out at. At the scheduler tip this tick measured (`origin/main` = `bf7427c8`) the ranges resolve; at any later tip nothing tells the reader which tree was meant.\n1. The paragraph (or its section header) names the scheduler commit the line numbers were measured at, in a form a reader can run (`git show <sha>:internal/api/lane_onboarding.go`), and a `grep -cE '[0-9a-f]{7,40}'` over that paragraph returns at least 1.\n2. Each cited range is re-derivable from the named commit: the functions the paragraph names (`laneIsUnarmed`, `validateLaneArming`, `laneAutoArm`) start on the first line of their cited ranges there, and the row note quotes the `grep -n 'func \\\\(laneIsUnarmed\\\\|validateLaneArming\\\\|laneAutoArm\\\\)'` output that shows it at that commit.\n3. The citation no longer ends exactly on the file's last line without saying so: `laneAutoArm`'s cited range `389-403` covers the whole tail of a 403-line file, so either the range is stated as \"to end of file, measured at <sha>\" or it is replaced by the function body's own boundaries.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0 at the fix commit (documentation-only change).","probe":"grep -c 'lane_onboarding\\.go' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md; grep -cE '(bf7427c8|scheduler@[0-9a-f]{7})' docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md; echo pin_census_done"}
+{"id":"CF-178","title":"P3 - the auto-disabled lane's durable pin still says enabled=true: the DB row, fleet.toml and the repo's lane loader disagree about a parked code-factory-qa","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T07:54:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"e1d0b4acacf35eb2fca789d3a436f2a186c4627b2e8b1f52bea80655f0ba962c"},"reasoning":"The paragraph's provenance section (docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md:142-147) tells the operator that a DB value has a durable ~/.hermes/fleet.toml pin behind it, and the same section is the one an operator uses to decide whether a live pacing value can be trusted. For code-factory-qa the two layers disagree and no record explains it: the live row is enabled=0 / disabled_by=auto-disable (disabled_at 2026-11-04T06:03:49Z, \"failure rate 91.3% (84/92 ticks, window 100, threshold 0.90)\"), while fleet.toml:2118 still reads enabled = true for the lane whose stanza is otherwise byte-current (cooldown_s = 1800, namespace code-factory).\n\nThe repo's only lane writer, scripts/load_lane_config.py, never reads disabled_at (0 occurrences), so nothing in the repo can notice that a lane it is about to configure has been parked by the scheduler, and the park exists only in the live DB. Whether a config reload actually re-enables the lane is NOT measured here and the row does not claim it; the measurable facts are the disagreement, the absence of a durable record of the park, and the writer that cannot see it. The fix is a decision recorded where the doc already promises one — which layer owns enabled after an auto-disable — plus a refusal in the writer so the park cannot be erased by a lane-config run.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: `grep -c 'disabled_at' scripts/load_lane_config.py` prints `0`; `sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name,enabled,disabled_by from projects where name='code-factory-qa'\"` prints `code-factory-qa|0|auto-disable`; `grep -A 10 'name = \"code-factory-qa\"' ~/.hermes/fleet.toml | grep -m1 enabled` prints `enabled = true` — the live row and its durable pin disagree, and nothing in the repo records the park.\n1. The repo states which layer owns `enabled` after an auto-disable and where the park is recorded: a documented decision in docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md naming the auto-disable fields (`disabled_at`, `disabled_by`, `disabled_reason`) and saying whether the durable `fleet.toml` pin is authoritative or is expected to go stale, so a reader can tell without guessing.\n2. After the change the two layers agree, and the row note quotes the re-run of acceptance criterion 0 showing one value for both. If the durable pin is authoritative, the lane is re-enabled and the note names the mechanism and the actor; otherwise the pin is flipped in the same change and the note names where that flip is recorded.\n3. `scripts/load_lane_config.py` refuses to write a lane whose row is parked (`disabled_by` non-empty) unless `--re-arm` is passed, so the only repo-side lane writer cannot silently restore a lane the scheduler parked; the refusal names the lane and writes nothing.\n4. Gate: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0, and the script change carries a selftest arm that goes red without the parked-lane refusal (fixture DB copy, parked row, no `--re-arm`).","probe":"grep -c 'disabled_at' scripts/load_lane_config.py; sqlite3 ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db \"select name, enabled, disabled_by from projects where name='code-factory-qa';\"; grep -A 10 'name = \"code-factory-qa\"' ~/.hermes/fleet.toml | grep -m1 enabled"}
